1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,245
For example, if we have the set with

2
00:00:02,345 --> 00:00:05,046
{1,2,3} the power set is the following set.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,346
So, it contains the individual elements,

4
00:00:07,466 --> 00:00:08,829
the pairs of elements,

5
00:00:08,933 --> 00:00:11,084
the whole set and the emptyset at the

6
00:00:11,184 --> 00:00:13,457
bottom, which is a subset of every set.

7
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:17,204
We will now use this to define the so-called Von Neuman Hierarchy of

8
00:00:17,304 --> 00:00:20,526
sets V, which is often considered to be THE Universe of sets.

9
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:23,314
Again, we start with the empty set, and then

10
00:00:23,414 --> 00:00:26,769
successively take the power set of the previous level.

11
00:00:26,933 --> 00:00:29,475
So we get the set with the emptyset at the first level, then

12
00:00:29,575 --> 00:00:32,292
the emptyset plus the set with the emptyset at the second level,

13
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,194
then the emptyset, set with the emptyset and then

14
00:00:35,294 --> 00:00:37,498
set with the set with the emptyset set.

15
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:40,341
This seems tame and boring, but this is a

16
00:00:40,441 --> 00:00:44,500
hyper-exponential function, the third level has 16 elements,

17
00:00:44,666 --> 00:00:47,657
the fourth level has 65 Thousand, and the fifth level

18
00:00:47,757 --> 00:00:50,339
has more atoms then there are in the Universe.

19
00:00:50,466 --> 00:00:54,777
Each level has the number of sets in the previous level raised to the

20
00:00:54,877 --> 00:00:58,804
power of 2. So, we can see how the complexity starts to emerge.

